;; TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; COMPILE-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv]: PASS
;; COMPILE-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv --differentiate]: PASS
;; SKIP-DEFAULT-PASS
;; HOIST-HARDWARE-PROFILE: bmg
;;
;; NO VERIFY-AUTODIFF HERE, AND THE REASON IS A HARNESS GAP, NOT A COMPILER ONE.
;; The first cut carried a VERIFY-AUTODIFF line reading
;;   (A=4x16@0:0.01 at.A=0,2 expect.A=4.0 group=64 h=0.5 atol=1e-3 output-mat=4x16)
;; and it read `analytical=1.0 numerical=0.0` against an expected 4.0.
;;
;; NB the directive above is deliberately NOT written out in full: directive parsing is
;; TEXTUAL and left-trims ";; ", so a quoted copy inside a comment is still parsed and the
;; check runs anyway.  Same trap as a CHECK-FAIL phrase that appears in its own filename.  The numerical 0.0 is
;; the tell: perturbing the input changed the objective not at all.
;;
;; The verify runner writes and reads every buffer as a 4-BYTE FLOAT
;; (tests/verify-autodiff-runner.lisp: "NIL, the default, means every input is 4-byte
;; float").  Endeavour 163 added a 2-byte path so `half` operands could be filled, but there
;; is no 8-byte path.  %vad-elem-bytes-of-type DOES answer 8 for double, so the buffer is
;; SIZED right and then filled with 4-byte floats -- the kernel reads a garbage double, the
;; finite difference sees nothing move, and the check fails for a reason that has nothing to
;; do with the derivative.  124/05, /06 and /11 differentiate doubles happily because they
;; use double CELLS, which take a different path from a matrix input.
;;
;; What still stands without it: this spec proves the 64-bit decomposition COMPILES under
;; --differentiate, 08 proves the same decomposition is numerically right on metal in the
;; forward direction, and 09 proves the xor VJP is numerically right on metal in float.  The
;; only untested corner is the double backward's NUMBERS, and it is untested because the
;; harness cannot express it.  Restore the directive above once the runner grows an 8-byte
;; path -- see F8 in shuffles.md.
;;
;; Endeavour 173 A|D: differentiating through the 64-bit hi/lo DECOMPOSITION.
;;
;; 09 in double.  The claim is that the two halves of the decomposition are not a special
;; case for the backward: adjoint(shuffle-xor v m) is shuffle-xor adj m whatever the width
;; of v, because the decomposition is below the level the AD rule is stated at.
;;
;; It is worth its own spec because the decomposition is the one place where a shuffle is
;; NOT a single instruction, and a backward written against "the shuffle instruction" rather
;; than against the shuffle OPERATION could plausibly transpose one half and not the other.
;; That failure is invisible in 09 (32-bit, one instruction) and invisible in 07/08 (forward
;; only, no adjoint to get wrong).
;;
;;     C[t] = w(t) * v[t^1]   =>   d sum(C)/dA[k] = w(k^1),   at k = 2: w(3) = 4.0
;;
;; Same arithmetic as 09, so a discrepancy between the two specs isolates the decomposition
;; as the cause rather than the AD rule.
;;
;; atol is TIGHTER than 09 (1e-3 vs 1e-2), which is the point of running it in double: if
;; this needs 09's tolerance to pass, something is being routed through float on the way.
;;
;; IF THIS FAILS FIRST, check the harness before the compiler: double-valued VERIFY-AUTODIFF
;; is exercised by 124/05, /06 and /11, but always on cells, never on a 4x16 matrix.

(def-type dmat-t (matrix double :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel diff_shuffle_double (A &out C)
  (declare #'(dmat-t &out dmat-t)
           (global-size :set-to 64)
           (local-size  :set-to 64))
  (let ((tid (to-int (get-local-linear-id))))
    ;; Crisp's LET is already sequential; let* compiles forward but breaks the AD walk.
    (let ((row (/ tid 16))
          (col (rem tid 16)))
      (let ((v (~ A row col))
            (w (to-double (+ col 1))))
        (set! (~ C row col) (* w (shuffle-xor v 1ul)))))))
